  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match melanoma PRS candidates are present (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743, PGS000766, PGS002246/PGS002247, PGS003430, PGS004247, PGS005208). However, many report only full-model AUROC/C-index with varying and sometimes heavy covariate sets (e.g., traditional risk factors/UV behaviors/family history), which the provided domain_knowledge flags as not directly comparable to standalone PRS performance when PRS-only metrics are absent. PGS000813 is selected because it is directly aligned to melanoma (familial cutaneous melanoma in non-carriers), reports strong discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.77, 0.75–0.79) and a strong per-SD effect size (OR 2.12, 1.9–2.35), providing clearer PRS signal than most alternatives in the visible set. The main limitation is endpoint specificity: it is in a familial, mutation-negative subgroup rather than a general-population melanoma endpoint, making it uncertain as the single best general melanoma PRS despite strong metrics; several general-population models exist but lack PRS-only metrics and/or have covariate-driven full-model performance, preventing a high-confidence winner."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 returned candidates are direct matches to the target trait (bladder/urinary bladder cancer; mostly EFO urinary bladder carcinoma) with generally endpoint-faithful phenotyping (e.g., “Cancer of bladder”, “Bladder cancer”, “Incident bladder cancer”; one uses “Urothelial carcinoma” which is closely related). However, across the pool there are no clearly reported PRS-only discrimination metrics; most reported AUROCs appear to be full-model and are not comparable as standalone PRS evidence per the provided domain guidance, which limits confidence.

Among the candidates with the most directly interpretable PRS signal, PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants; UK Biobank development; European evaluation) reports a relatively strong and precise per-SD effect size (OR=1.284, 95% CI 1.211–1.361) and an extreme-tail contrast (OR top 1% vs rest = 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93), with endpoint “Cancer of bladder” and only basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4). It is also supported by a reasonable full-model AUROC (~0.567) that is not driven by heavy clinical covariate packaging.

Key limitation: several near-clone alternatives in the same endpoint family show very similar full-model AUROC and per-SD OR (e.g., PGS000610/612/613) and one large multi-cohort incident model (PGS000071) reports a much higher full-model AUROC (~0.803) but that metric is likely covariate-driven and not PRS-comparable; thus the evidence is insufficient to claim a single clearly best, high-quality direct-match choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported are consistently bladder cancer/"cancer of bladder"; one is urothelial carcinoma but still within the same disease spectrum). However, across the pool there are no clearly PRS-comparable metrics (no PGS-only AUROC/R2), and many reported AUROCs are full-model values that are not directly comparable across differing covariate sets (e.g., age/sex/smoking/BMI vs basic PCs), limiting confidence.

Among the most endpoint-faithful and comparably evaluated options, the Cancer PRSweb ‘GWAS Hits’ family (PGS000610/611/612/613) reports similar full-model AUROC (~0.57) but PGS000611 stands out with the strongest available high-risk stratification signal (OR top 1% vs rest = 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93) alongside a per-SD OR of 1.284 (1.211–1.361), and uses only basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4). This gives it the best-supported PRS-specific evidence within a set of otherwise near-clone endpoints.

Key limitation: the selected evidence is still based on full-model-style metrics and effect sizes without explicit PRS-only discrimination, and other candidates (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782) show higher full-model AUROC but with heavier epidemiologic covariates, making their apparent advantage potentially covariate-driven rather than PRS-driven per the provided domain guidance."}

